  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Given that $y=2{x}^{3}-2x$ for $x=x(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1$ at the point where $x=-2,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{x}^{3}-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{x}^{3}-2x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-2x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6{x}^{2}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $x=-2,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\cdot (6\\cdot (-2)^{2}-2)=22.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
